A||||10||theogre||some background
Z||000000||theogre||04-07-99||10:00 PM||||I've been a "dual major" in both cars, and electronics/computers for about 20+ years. I am actually rather new to Fiero specifically, but I've worked on other GM/Pontiac/Olds with these same engines. I am currently writing some HTML for a new section to my site that will include some unique, hopefully, information on how to fix some parts of the car.<p>I know I tend to write long answers. Sorry if that bothers anyone. I've found that not everyone understands how some parts relate to others, and I'm trying to make sure all readers of this service can take away usefull data.<p>Sometimes I write what seem like crabby answers. Sorry about that. Sometimes I'm tired, sometimes the point of a thread gets muddy. Please don't acuse me of "flaming." Ranting maybe, but never flaming.<p>My overall feeling about Fiero is this: It is what it is. As anyone who's looked at this car over the years, or anyone who's searched the web these days, has seen..... the Fiero is an amazing collection of other cars, with a tremendous amount of new technology thrown in. What amazes me is how good an end result the severly constrained Pontiac designers got. For all it's problems it's still an incredible piece of work, and stands up even to many of todays cars.<p>I have an 87 SC with the 4 cyl auto package. I get 27 mixed, and 32 highway MPG. I can't think of any other car built at that time with this, or similar motor, that got any thing like that. Hell, alot of cars today don't get that good.<p>There is technology on Fiero that only now is making itself common on other cars. I actually got to see some of the DuPont testing of some of the cars features a few years ago. DuPont was testing these features several years after the car was extinct from production. Most people don't know what a headache it was to come up with a finish that would be uniform in appearance when applied to several diferant materials. Especially as the finnish aged.
Z||000001||Patrick||04-08-99||12:20 AM||||As someone who has just discovered this site in the last few days, all I can say is "<b>Thanks</b>" for any and all of the time you put in here.<p>It's probably a good time and place to thank Cliff for the site also.<p>As much as I enjoy my '87 Fiero, it has also got to be the most frustating car I've ever owned. A lot of the frustration has to do with simply the "computerization" of any modern automobile. My "newest" vehicle before I bought the Fiero just over two years ago was a 1978 Z28 Camaro. No computer in that car, just a lot of horses! I was never stumped with a problem on that car, nor on the three previous high performance Chevs that I've had. There was never anything that a bigger hammer wouldn't cure. (Just kidding!)<p>Anyway, thanks again to anyone and everyone who contributes to this site.<p>-Patrick
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||04-08-99||02:54 PM||||Well Ogre, I for one am very grateful for your long answers!<p>My Fiero background you ask (ok, you didn't but here goes)?<p>I first saw the Fiero on a trip to the States in 1986 and fell in love with it almost immediately. I never cared much for other sportscars like Porches, Ferrari's, or whatever but the Fiero had that "je ne sais quoi" that made me love it instantly. 3 Years later I bought my first Fiero when my work needed me to move to the USA for a year (San Antonio). It was a black '85 GT notchback and I took it with me to Holland. It was the 15th Fiero ever to be imported here. The car was so unique, I got an offer for around $25,000 dollar for it within a week (seriously!).<p>The 86.5 GT I currently own is my third Fiero and although there are perhaps around a thousand Fiero's now in Holland, it still is a unique and quite expensive car here. Mine was around $10,000 and although that is very expensive for U.S. standards, it's not all that out-of-the-ordinary here. Cars are really expensive in Holland. New cars start at around $10,000 here, and that will get you perhaps the smallest Yugo! For instance, a new Pontiac Trans Sport will set you back for around $40,000. We also pay around $400 road-taxes, gas is $4.- a gallon, insurance is expensive, etc. So owning a car here is <b>really</b> expensive...
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||04-08-99||02:57 PM||||Oh, and I really think the Fiero is a <b>design classic</b>. When I tell someone that my car is 13 years old, nobody believes me. It still turns heads and not a lot of other cars with that age do that. 
Z||000004||Pretender||04-08-99||04:25 PM||||I've been the owner of my 88GT for 4 years, i was way in over my head when i bought it, first the insurance killed me to the point where i had to find a cheaper ride (89' conquest) and i did have it highly modified to around the lower 13 sec. mark untill the 86 yrl. woman pulled out in front on my completely totaling my baby., at this point the fiero has been sitting in my backyard for 3 years so i had to resurect it, not an easy task, you name it i had to buy it, even the oil pan rusted through!('nother story!)..after the 2k my car was up and running but it's soo slow...i've been putting some performance stuff on it., maybe one day i'll be able to stand tall upagainst those damn neon rt's!...one of them here is running into the low 14's!...*sigh*<p><br>
Z||000005||Motorhead||04-08-99||05:11 PM||||I just had to jump in here!  I bought my first Fiero in 1984 brand new off the showroom floor. I can't tell you how I felt driving one of the first! The car turned ALOT of heads back then, in particular, female!<p>At the tender age of 20 years, I found this to be an applaudible attribute. The girls fell all over the car.<p>However after driving the car frequently between Myrtle Beach, SC and Philly, PA on that straight,normally boring stretch of I95 at speeds exceeding 100 MPH for hours at a time, I fell in love.<p>I have restored and autocrossed English 2-seaters for years such as, MG, Triumph TR6, TR 250, and Sunbeam Alpine before and since my first Fiero, but the Pontiac was allways in the back of my mind.<p>I now own a 1986 2.5 coupe and a 1983 Datsun 280ZX turbo. The Fiero is the car of choice for those winding country roads, the Z car is the 5.0 Mustang killer, IN A STRAIGHT LINE!!<p>I have found that cars are like women," they all gratify in their own way".<p>Thanks for this forum Chris, and thanks for your contributions Ogre!<p>Looking forward to a long and "gratifying" relationship with my latest acquisition, FIERO!   <p>  
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||04-08-99||05:23 PM||||Did you just call me <i>Chris</i>???  ;)
Z||000007||Motorhead||04-08-99||06:31 PM||||Sorry CLIFF!!<p>Happy Fieroing!
Z||000008||kel||04-13-99||03:34 PM||||I too just found this site a couple of days ago, and have really enjoyed it.  Ogre, I don't mind your long answers.  You've helped me to learn a few things. In fact, just about everyone has.  I wasn't sure at first if a "girl" could fit in, but it's been fun,even though I just "eavesdrop" most of the time.  I do know this, I LOVE my '86 Fiero SE 2M6 that I ordered new way back in Oct.'85 (paid about $12,100....the sticker price was $14,400) and it's good to know I'm not the only one (sometimes you feel that way) that appreciates what absolute fun it is to drive!
Z||000009||Patrick||04-13-99||03:44 PM||||A "girl" at this site? Does this mean the rest of us now have to bathe and watch our language? :)<p>Welcome Kel, there's plenty of us newbies here.
Z||000010||kel||04-14-99||02:20 AM||||Thanks Patrick<br>I promise, you won't have to use soap to bathe or wash your mouths out!    :)<p>
